== Fifth generation (KC1/2; 2014) ==
{{Hatnote|See [[Acura RLX]] for information on the North American model.}}
[[File:Honda Legend 3.5 P-AWS KC2 Super Platinum Silver Metallic (1) (cropped).jpg|thumb|2015 Honda Legend]]

The fifth generation Legend was unveiled in Japan on November 10, 2014, and went on sale on February 20, 2015, it is near identical to the [[Acura RLX]] and only available in Sport Hybrid. The facelifted model was unveiled in Japan on October 2, 2017, and launched at the [[Tokyo Motor Show#2017|Tokyo Motor Show]] on October 27, 2017.

===Facelift===
{{Multiple image | align = left | direction = vertical | width = 220
| image1 = 2018 Honda Legend.jpg
| image2 = 2018 Honda Legend rear.jpg
| caption2 = 2018 Honda Legend
}}
The front fascia of the facelifted model is based from the facelifted RLX. It is equipped with HONDA Sensing which now includes Traffic Jam Assist (Congestion Driving Support Function) which supports steering, acceleration, braking, and camera/sensor harmony when congested in traffic jams.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.honda.co.jp/LEGEND/webcatalog/performance/active-safety/ |title = 予防安全性能｜性能・安全｜レジェンド｜Honda}}</ref> The length grew to 5,032&nbsp;mm, making it the longest Honda/Acura sedan to date, with length exceeding 5,000&nbsp;mm.
{{clear}}

===Discontinued sales of RLX===
2020 was the final model year for the North American [[Acura RLX]] due to low sales, with no direct successor planned.<ref name="motor1">{{Cite web |url= https://www.motor1.com/news/423393/acura-rlx-sedan-dead/ |title= Acura RLX Sedan Dead In North America After 2020 Model Year|work=[[Motorsport Network|motor1.com]] |last= Smith| first= Christopher|date=14 May 2020|access-date=15 May 2020}}</ref>

===Hybrid EX===
On March 5, 2021, Honda began lease sales in Japan of the all-new Legend equipped with "[[Collision avoidance system|Honda SENSING Elite]]", which is a variation of Honda SENSING, a suite of advanced safety and driver-assistive technologies currently available for Honda vehicles around the world. One of the “elite” technologies is the “Traffic Jam Pilot” function, an advanced technology qualifying for Level 3 [[Self-driving car|automated driving]] (conditional automated driving in limited area).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://global.honda/newsroom/news/2021/4210304eng-legend.html |date=March 4, 2021 |title=Honda to Begin Sales of Legend with New Honda SENSING Elite |website=[[Honda]] |access-date=March 6, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Beresford |first=Colin |url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a35729591/honda-legend-level-3-autonomy-leases-japan/ |date=March 4, 2021 |title=Honda Legend Sedan with Level 3 Autonomy Available for Lease in Japan |website=[[Car and Driver]] |access-date=March 11, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url= https://japan-forward.com/honda-launches-worlds-first-level-3-self-driving-car/ |author=Takafumi Uno |date=March 9, 2021 |title=Honda Launches World's First Level-3 Self-driving Car |website=Japan Forward |access-date=March 18, 2021 }}</ref>

===Discontinuation===
In June 2021, it was announced that the Sayama plant that produces the Legend would be closed at the end of 2021 and the current car would not have a successor.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Gauthier |first1=Michael |title=Honda Kills Legend And Odyssey In Japan, America's Minivan Not Impacted |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2021/06/death-of-jdm-honda-odyssey-wont-impact-us-spec-model/ |access-date=21 June 2021}}</ref> With the discontinuation, [[Honda Accord]] is once again the largest Honda sedan.

